# Binary subtraction. Two numbers, most significant bit first.
#
#   rw progs/binsub.rw '<1011-110>'   ->   101
#   rw progs/binsub.rw '<110-1011>'   ->   -101
#
# binadd.rw with the sign flipped: same layout, same travellers, P and Q now
# carry a borrow rather than a carry.
#
#   < left operand | P | answer | separator | right operand | >
#
# Going negative is the only part addition did not need. The columns produce
# the answer modulo two to the width, so when the borrow falls out of the top
# the digits on the tape are the two's complement of what was wanted. N walks
# the answer flipping every bit and C adds the one back, which turns it into
# the magnitude, and the minus sign is not written until the final rewrite --
# the rule that starts this program has "-" on its left-hand side, so a sign
# sitting on the tape any earlier would be read as another operator.

-->P|

# a traveller reaches the marker: one column.
# left bit, carried bit, borrow in; result bit and borrow out.
0PT->P0
1PT->P1
0PU->Q1
1PU->P0
0QT->Q1
1QT->P0
0QU->Q0
1QU->Q1

# the left operand is spent, so its bit is a 0
<PT-><P0
<PU-><Q1
<QT-><Q1
<QU-><Q0

# a traveller on its way left
0T->T0
1T->T1
|T->T|
0U->U0
1U->U1
|U->U|

# launch: take the last bit of the right operand
0>->T>
1>->U>

# right operand spent: ripple the borrow through what is left of the left one
0Q->Q1
1Q->P0
0P->P0
1P->P1

# the borrow fell out of the top, so the answer is negative and the digits are
# its two's complement. Flip them and add one to get the magnitude.
<Q->{PN
N0->1N
N1->0N
N|->C|
0C->1
1C->C0
PC->P1

# drop leading zeros, but only while two digits remain
P00->P0
P01->P1

# scaffolding off, sign last
|>->
<P->.
{P->.-
